**Theorem 7.1 (uniform rank-one exclusion).** Under Assumption 1.1,
there are no data in Assumption 1.3 with

$$
t\ge\max(t_0,2000000000),\qquad
R_-=-\rho ww^T,\quad \rho>0,\quad\|w\|=1.
\tag{RB.39}
$$

The quantifiers cover every finite positive multiplier, every finite
entire-cylinder feasible dual and every finite common law jointly
attaining the same $C_t(\lambda)$, throughout that finite range.

Proof. Suppose such data exist. This range meets the small-mass,
multiplier and denominator-absorption thresholds simultaneously.
Propositions 4.1, 5.1 and 6.1 give

$$
\begin{aligned}
M_P
&<5+\frac{25}{2}(20000)
       +\frac{150(64000000)}{2000000000}\\
&=\frac{1250049}{5}=250009.8<250010.
\end{aligned}
\tag{RB.40}
$$

Now take the exact unit direction $x_\dagger=(24/25,7/25)$.
FC11.1 applies to its positive first coordinate, so
$\ell(x_\dagger)>0$. Its failure height, using both bounds in
(RB.5), satisfies

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x_\dagger)
&=\frac{336e-49d_*}{625}\\
&>\frac{(3/200)168-(11/500)49}{625}
=\frac{721}{312500}>\frac1{500}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{RB.41}
$$

At this direction the positive-report, positive-height saturated
signed test is globally nonnegative, whether or not the direction
is occupied in any primal law. Substituting (RB.6) in that test
gives exactly

$$
\begin{aligned}
P(x_\dagger)\ge{}&
\ell(x_\dagger)+\lambda(24/25)
+\rho c(x_\dagger)(w\cdot x_\dagger)^2
+2t c(x_\dagger)f(x_\dagger)\\
>{}&\frac{t}{6250}\ge320000.
\end{aligned}
\tag{RB.42}
$$

Here $c>1/25$, $f>1/500$, $\lambda>0$, and the squared term is
nonnegative for both orientations of $w$. Since
$P(x_\dagger)\le M_P$, (RB.42) contradicts (RB.40).
No occupied, unused, reverse, negative or paired group was removed
to obtain either bound. $\square$

